1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,304
Monday, seven in the morning.

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:06,936
Timur stands in the bank's server room, in front of a fresh server.

3
00:00:06,986 --> 00:00:11,568
Today he installs the card zone, and five colleagues stand behind him.

4
00:00:11,618 --> 00:00:14,664
Dilnoza from operations is one of them.

5
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:20,064
In Lesson 6, every key and setting in the system came from Vault.

6
00:00:20,114 --> 00:00:25,128
In Lesson 9, card numbers were sealed with keys that never leave Vault.

7
00:00:25,178 --> 00:00:28,584
This lesson is about who can open Vault itself.

8
00:00:28,634 --> 00:00:31,992
There is an easier way, and Timur knows it.

9
00:00:32,042 --> 00:00:37,920
Run an install script once, and keep the master password in a text file on his laptop.

10
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:40,584
Months later, Timur leaves the bank.

11
00:00:40,634 --> 00:00:42,456
Then a disk fails.

12
00:00:42,506 --> 00:00:48,888
The database backup is there, but nobody can read it, because nobody kept the keys that sealed it.

13
00:00:48,938 --> 00:00:54,312
One person and one file: the keys end up either lost or leaked.

14
00:00:54,362 --> 00:00:59,904
Most teams guard their keys against strangers, and forget the person who installed them.

15
00:00:59,954 --> 00:01:02,880
I think that person is the bigger risk.

16
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:08,960
So how can a bank open Vault without trusting one person?

17
00:01:09,010 --> 00:01:11,240
So Vault opens a different way.

18
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:16,472
At install, Vault's opening key is split into five key shares.

19
00:01:16,522 --> 00:01:21,056
A key share is one piece of that key, useless on its own.

20
00:01:21,106 --> 00:01:24,032
Any three shares together open Vault.

21
00:01:24,082 --> 00:01:27,272
No one alone can, and no two can either.

22
00:01:27,322 --> 00:01:31,040
Each share goes to one person: a keyholder.

23
00:01:31,090 --> 00:01:36,152
A keyholder keeps their share in their own password manager, and nowhere else.

24
00:01:36,202 --> 00:01:41,024
The bank chooses the keyholders, and makes sure no one holds two shares.

25
00:01:41,074 --> 00:01:48,896
Nothing of this ceremony is written down anywhere: not the shares, not on the server, not in a log.

26
00:01:48,946 --> 00:01:51,032
Vault also locks itself.

27
00:01:51,082 --> 00:01:57,032
Every time it starts, it starts sealed: it holds everything, but can read nothing.

28
00:01:57,082 --> 00:02:02,624
To unseal it, three keyholders come, and each types their own share.

29
00:02:02,674 --> 00:02:06,080
Until then, nothing that needs Vault can run.

30
00:02:06,130 --> 00:02:10,976
Setting things up needs one more key: a master key for the session.

31
00:02:11,026 --> 00:02:18,488
It is made from three keyholders' shares, used for that one session, and always revoked at the end.

32
00:02:18,538 --> 00:02:23,192
So no master key sits around, waiting to be stolen.

33
00:02:23,242 --> 00:02:25,064
And backups.

34
00:02:25,114 --> 00:02:28,784
Card numbers in the database are sealed by Vault's keys.

35
00:02:28,834 --> 00:02:33,056
A database backup without that Vault is unreadable.

36
00:02:33,106 --> 00:02:38,360
So the database and Vault are always backed up together, and restored together.

37
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:42,776
Back in the server room, Timur starts the card zone's installer.

38
00:02:42,826 --> 00:02:46,592
It checks the machine first, then reaches the keys.

39
00:02:46,642 --> 00:02:55,184
It says Vault is new, and that each of the five keyholders copies their own share straight into their own password manager.

40
00:02:55,234 --> 00:02:59,336
Then it waits: press Enter when the keyholders are here.

41
00:02:59,386 --> 00:03:01,208
Timur presses Enter.

42
00:03:01,258 --> 00:03:05,816
From here, the camera stays on the people, never on the screen.

43
00:03:05,866 --> 00:03:08,480
Each keyholder saves their own share.

44
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:12,920
The installer waits again, until every share is saved.

45
00:03:12,970 --> 00:03:14,792
Then it clears the screen.

46
00:03:14,842 --> 00:03:25,208
Three keyholders step up, one at a time, and type their shares unseen: first to unseal Vault, then to make the session's master key.

47
00:03:25,258 --> 00:03:29,696
The installer sets everything up and starts the services.

48
00:03:29,746 --> 00:03:32,984
At the end, it revokes the master key.

49
00:03:33,034 --> 00:03:36,872
Then Timur does what every bank should do on day one.

50
00:03:36,922 --> 00:03:40,304
He restarts Vault, just as any reboot would.

51
00:03:40,354 --> 00:03:42,392
He runs the installer's check.

52
00:03:42,442 --> 00:03:47,624
It stops at once: Vault is sealed, and nothing that needs it can run.

53
00:03:47,674 --> 00:03:49,832
Three keyholders are called.

54
00:03:49,882 --> 00:03:53,600
Dilnoza types her share first, unseen.

55
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:55,472
Two more follow.

56
00:03:55,522 --> 00:03:59,816
On the third share, Vault opens, and the check passes.

57
00:03:59,866 --> 00:04:01,688
Nine o'clock.

58
00:04:01,738 --> 00:04:06,848
No one in that room can open the keys alone, and no one can hold them hostage.

59
00:04:06,898 --> 00:04:13,376
If any one of them leaves, loses a laptop, or is tempted, the bank loses nothing.

60
00:04:13,426 --> 00:04:17,672
A stolen server or a stolen backup holds nothing readable.

61
00:04:17,722 --> 00:04:22,808
And as long as the database and Vault are backed up together, a restore works.

62
00:04:22,858 --> 00:04:30,656
To recap: Vault's opening key is split into five shares, and any three keyholders open it.

63
00:04:30,706 --> 00:04:34,888
So can Timur open Vault alone?

64
00:04:34,938 --> 00:04:38,536
No: it always takes three keyholders.

65
00:04:38,586 --> 00:04:41,320
Next: one install, everywhere.
